# If you prefer the allow list template instead of the deny list, see community template:
# https://github.com/github/gitignore/blob/main/community/Golang/Go.AllowList.gitignore
#
# Binaries for programs and plugins
*.exe
*.exe~
*.dll
*.so
*.dylib

# Test binary, built with `go test -c`
*.test

# Code coverage profiles and other test artifacts
*.out
coverage.*
*.coverprofile
profile.cov

# Dependency directories (remove the comment below to include it)
vendor/


# Go workspace file
go.work
go.work.sum

# Environment secrets
.env
.env.local
.env.*.local

# Editor/IDE
# .idea/
.vscode/

# Python virtual environment
.venv

#config
config.json
config.yaml

# The web application's runtime config is a build input, not an operator's local
# gateway config. It is the same filename, so the bare pattern above swallowed
# it: a clean checkout shipped a dist with no config.json, loadRuntimeConfig
# threw HTTP 404, and the SPA rendered its fatal-error screen instead of
# mounting. The committed file carries the same-origin default the README
# documents, and the gateway now serves the dashboard same-origin, so the
# default is also the deployed value.
!web/public/config.json

# binaries — `make build` writes ./bin/ferrogw.
#
# Anchored with a leading slash on purpose. Unanchored, `bin/` matches a
# directory of that name at ANY depth, which silently swallowed
# packaging/npm/bin/ferrogw.js — the npm package's entire entry point — so the
# published package would have had no shim and `npx ferrogw` would have failed
# with nothing in the repository to explain it. Only ./bin is a build output.
/bin/
# `go build ./cmd/ferrogw` with no -o writes the binary here, and that command
# is in the docs as the way to check a build without Node.
/ferrogw

# Database files
*.db

#git hooks
.githooks

# Playwright test artifacts
.playwright/
.playwright-mcp/

# Local scratch and planning notes, not part of the distribution.
.superpowers/
docs/superpowers/

# Stray npm/vitest artifacts if a web command is run from the repo root.
node_modules/

# The get.ferrolabs.ai Worker inlines scripts/install/ into its bundle at deploy
# time, so this file is a copy of two tracked scripts. Committing it would give
# the installers a second home that can silently disagree with the first — and
# it is the copy the edge would actually serve. CI builds it from a fresh
# checkout; only a local `wrangler dev` leaves it (and .wrangler/) behind.
deploy/get-worker/scripts.generated.js
deploy/get-worker/.wrangler/

# The dashboard bundle is compiled into the binary with go:embed, so it has to
# live inside the package directory — go:embed cannot reach outside it. The
# build copies web/dist here. Only .gitkeep is committed, which keeps the embed
# pattern resolvable in a fresh clone; the bundle itself is generated, and
# nothing tracked lives here for a build to dirty.
internal/webui/dist/*
!internal/webui/dist/.gitkeep

# GoReleaser build output.
/dist/

# Generated at release time by scripts/gen-licenses.sh (kept out of dist/ so
# GoReleaser's empty-dist guard is satisfied); bundled into archives + image.
/notices/
/.agents/
/.codex/
